WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:20.680
Hello! Welcome back to Superstars in History—the talk show that features famous people from the past.

2
00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:28.786
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and I've had a dream about interviewing today's guest ever since I launched

3
00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:29.767
this show.

4
00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:40.112
A pastor, civil rights leader, and powerful speaker, this man changed history with peaceful protests and stirring speeches.

5
00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:46.135
Please join me in welcoming a personal hero Martin Luther King Jr.!

6
00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:05.016
Thank you, Faye.

7
00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:07.518
Let's get started, Dr.

8
00:01:07.558 --> 00:01:14.322
King! You were born on January 15, 1929, in Atlanta, Georgia.

9
00:01:14.942 --> 00:01:16.643
Did you have a happy childhood?

10
00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:21.545
Indeed I did, thanks to two marvelous parents who filled our home with love.

11
00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:27.689
My father was a pastor at Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta, so I grew up listening to his

12
00:01:27.749 --> 00:01:33.232
sermons, witnessing firsthand the power of words to move and inspire people.

13
00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:39.179
Meanwhile my mother taught me that everyone is worthy; nobody is inferior to anyone else.

14
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:45.302
Those were probably important lessons, since you grew up in the segregated South!

15
00:01:46.203 --> 00:01:49.045
Yes, it wasn't an easy place for a black child to grow up.

16
00:01:49.705 --> 00:01:57.229
Slavery was abolished when the Civil War ended in 1865, but African Americans still weren't treated equally, especially

17
00:01:57.269 --> 00:01:57.749
in the South.

18
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:06.830
There were laws that made all kinds of racial discrimination legal, and segregation was everywhere: restaurants, movie theaters,

19
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:14.013
beaches, parks I couldn't swim in a city pool or drink from certain water fountains.

20
00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:20.796
When I was six years old, a white playmate I had known forever suddenly wouldn't play with me

21
00:02:20.876 --> 00:02:23.217
anymore because he was starting at a white school.

22
00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.819
Not even children's schools were outside the bondage of segregation!

23
00:02:28.679 --> 00:02:33.683
When you were 14, you won a prize for writing a speech about segregation.

24
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:35.984
Yes, I'm proud of that award.

25
00:02:36.525 --> 00:02:39.687
But the occasion was marred by what happened on the trip home.

26
00:02:40.447 --> 00:02:45.691
The bus driver made my teacher and me give up our seats to white passengers— we had to

27
00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:47.993
stand in the aisle for ninety miles!

28
00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:52.116
That was the angriest I ever was during my lifetime!

29
00:02:53.057 --> 00:02:54.077
How awful!

30
00:02:55.578 --> 00:02:57.500
Did you go to college after high school?

31
00:02:58.792 --> 00:03:02.733
I graduated high school at 15 and then attended a black college.

32
00:03:03.453 --> 00:03:07.795
I decided to become a minister like my father, so I went to a seminary in the North

33
00:03:08.035 --> 00:03:10.755
after college and eventually earned a Ph.D.

34
00:03:11.395 --> 00:03:19.358
During my studies I became fascinated by Mohandas Gandhi, the activist in India who advocated nonviolent protest as

35
00:03:19.418 --> 00:03:20.958
a way to reform society.

36
00:03:21.958 --> 00:03:27.620
I began incorporating Gandhi's ideas into my approach to fighting for civil rights.

37
00:03:28.856 --> 00:03:32.099
In 1954 you moved to Alabama with your wife.

38
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.825
After living in the integrated North, why did you want to return to the segregated South?

39
00:03:39.705 --> 00:03:45.290
Well, I was hired to serve as the pastor at Dexter Avenue Baptist Church in Montgomery, and I

40
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.052
had a hope I could fight segregation in my position.

41
00:03:48.913 --> 00:03:52.816
The following year I did indeed help to lead a large protest in Montgomery.

42
00:03:53.877 --> 00:03:57.580
Oh! You're talking about the Montgomery Bus Boycott!

43
00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:04.287
After Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing to give up her seat to a white person, African Americans

44
00:04:04.427 --> 00:04:07.669
boycotted the city buses for over a year!

45
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.832
We met at my church to organize the boycott, and I served as a spokesperson.

46
00:04:13.372 --> 00:04:17.455
It was exciting to be involved in the movement but also dangerous.

47
00:04:18.635 --> 00:04:24.018
I received death threats, and one night while I was out at a meeting, my home was bombed.

48
00:04:24.959 --> 00:04:25.519
Oh no!

49
00:04:26.059 --> 00:04:27.020
Was anyone hurt?

50
00:04:28.308 --> 00:04:29.109
Fortunately, no.

51
00:04:30.129 --> 00:04:38.514
In November 1956 the boycott finally ended when the Supreme Court ruled that segregated bus laws were unconstitutional.

52
00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:42.056
We'd won a victory, Faye, but there was more work to be done.

53
00:04:42.956 --> 00:04:49.360
I traveled around the country supporting folks in other boycotts and speaking about nonviolent protest.

54
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:56.224
Speaking of nonviolent protest, you participated in many sit-ins at lunch counters.

55
00:04:56.736 --> 00:04:58.116
What can you tell us about them?

56
00:04:58.957 --> 00:05:05.218
In the sixties students began holding sit-ins at lunch counters where only white people were served.

57
00:05:05.818 --> 00:05:09.179
They'd sit down and quietly demand service.

58
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.181
White people sometimes threw food or taunted them, but the protestors just sat there in silence.

59
00:05:16.221 --> 00:05:20.462
Those students were a glowing example of nonviolent protest.

60
00:05:21.323 --> 00:05:24.403
You got arrested at a sit-in or march, right?

61
00:05:25.684 --> 00:05:32.704
Yes, by then I had moved back to Atlanta to serve as a co-pastor at Ebenezer Baptist Church.

62
00:05:32.804 --> 00:05:39.269
In 1963 I traveled to Birmingham, Alabama, where black folks were staging sit-ins and marches.

63
00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:44.713
When I was arrested after joining a protest, I wrote a letter from my jail cell that described

64
00:05:44.873 --> 00:05:46.494
our philosophy of nonviolence.

65
00:05:47.174 --> 00:05:51.097
I felt I had to write the letter—white ministers were calling us lawbreakers.

66
00:05:52.058 --> 00:05:59.145
I wanted to explain that nonviolence is a powerful and just weapon—it draws attention to injustices.

67
00:06:00.205 --> 00:06:02.946
Your letter from jail is really famous.

68
00:06:03.687 --> 00:06:08.368
But it's probably not as famous as your "I Have a Dream" speech!

69
00:06:09.308 --> 00:06:11.069
When did you make that speech?

70
00:06:12.329 --> 00:06:19.171
Back in August 1963, I helped to organize the March for Jobs and Freedom, which attracted over a

71
00:06:19.471 --> 00:06:21.552
quarter-million people to Washington, DC.

72
00:06:22.068 --> 00:06:25.850
We were marching for equality for all people, regardless of race or gender.

73
00:06:26.711 --> 00:06:33.334
Most folks remember this part of my speech: I have a dream that my four little children will

74
00:06:33.515 --> 00:06:38.878
one day live in a nation where they will not be judged by the color of their skin—

75
00:06:39.538 --> 00:06:42.139
but by the content of their character!

76
00:06:45.121 --> 00:06:47.322
I practically know the speech by heart!

77
00:06:48.103 --> 00:06:49.764
It made you super famous.

78
00:06:50.592 --> 00:06:57.494
You were named Time magazine's Man of the Year, and the following year, you won the Nobel Peace

79
00:06:57.594 --> 00:07:04.816
Prize. Yes, and the March on Washington led the government to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964,

80
00:07:05.356 --> 00:07:08.757
which made segregation illegal in the United States.

81
00:07:09.797 --> 00:07:13.358
After that I focused on international peace and economic justice.

82
00:07:14.118 --> 00:07:19.100
I spoke out about the Vietnam War and worked with the Poor People's Campaign, a coalition of poor

83
00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.029
Americans advocating for economic change.

84
00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:30.211
My final fight for justice came in 1968 when I traveled to Memphis, Tennessee, to march with sanitation

85
00:07:30.251 --> 00:07:31.891
workers striking for better pay.

86
00:07:33.172 --> 00:07:36.372
Perhaps you could share the rest of that story without me?

87
00:07:38.413 --> 00:07:39.593
Of course, Dr.

88
00:07:39.653 --> 00:07:44.875
King. Thank you for joining us—we'll let you return to history now!

89
00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:48.996
Remember the power of peaceful protest!

90
00:07:49.632 --> 00:07:59.077
Good-bye! On April 4, 1968, during that trip to Memphis, Dr.

91
00:07:59.137 --> 00:08:02.119
King stepped out onto the balcony of his hotel.

92
00:08:02.799 --> 00:08:07.582
Tragically he was assassinated by a man who supported segregation.

93
00:08:09.463 --> 00:08:16.307
Dr. King was only 39 years old when he died, but he had already accomplished so many things

94
00:08:16.387 --> 00:08:18.028
in his fight for civil rights.

95
00:08:18.872 --> 00:08:25.856
Today his dream of equality for all people lives on, both in his famous speeches and in civil

96
00:08:25.936 --> 00:08:27.897
rights movements around the world.

97
00:08:28.758 --> 00:08:29.919
Thank you for joining me.

98
00:08:30.279 --> 00:08:34.701
I'll be back soon to interview another superstar from the past!
